Sure! Here’s how you can use the `getActiveDataEntries` function to pull out all items whose `data-active` attribute is set to `true`.

> **NOTE** – The function expects the *list element* to be passed as a **stringified** JavaScript object (i.e., JSON). If you already have a JavaScript array/object, just `JSON.stringify()` it before calling the function.

```javascript
// Example list element (array of objects)
const listElement = [
  { id: 1, name: "Item A", "data-active": true },
  { id: 2, name: "Item B", "data-active": false },
  { id: 3, name: "Item C", "data-active": true }
];

// Convert the list element to a JSON string
const listElementString = JSON.stringify(listElement);

// Call the function
const activeEntries = getActiveDataEntries({
  listElement: listElementString,
  attribute: "data-active",   // optional – defaults to "data-active"
  value: true                 // optional – defaults to true
});

console.log(activeEntries);
// Output will be:
// [
//   { id: 1, name: "Item A", "data-active": true },
//   { id: 3, name: "Item C", "data-active": true }
// ]
```

### Quick Recap

| Parameter | Type | Description | Default |
|-----------|------|-------------|---------|
| `listElement` | `string` | JSON‑stringified list element (array of objects) | N/A |
| `attribute` | `string` | Attribute to filter on | `"data-active"` |
| `value` | `boolean` | Value to match | `true` |

Feel free to adjust the `attribute` and `value` if you need to filter on something else. Let me know if you run into any issues!
